<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free State of Thuringia</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1952 CE · capital Weimar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Union of seven small Thuringian states in 1920, which saw the first Nazi minister and later disappeared into East German districts.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After 1918 the small Ernestine and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-schwarzburg">Schwarzburg</a> states and Reuss sought union. On 1 May 1920 Saxe-Weimar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>, Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>, Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-schwarzburg-rudolstadt">Schwarzburg-Rudolstadt</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-schwarzburg-sondershausen">Schwarzburg-Sondershausen</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peoples-state-of-reuss">People's State of Reuss</a> merged into the Land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> as capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> instead.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> had textile, toy, glass and optical industries, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-zeiss-ag">Carl Zeiss</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>. In 1923 a left-wing government was removed by the federal army. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> in 1925 under nationalist pressure. In 1930 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-frick">Wilhelm Frick</a> became Thuringia's interior minister, the first Nazi to hold a ministerial post. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buchenwald-concentration-camp">Buchenwald concentration camp</a> was built near Weimar in 1937.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> was in the Soviet zone and became a Land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a>. In 1952 it was divided into the districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, Gera and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suhl">Suhl</a>; the state was re-established in 1990.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-state-of-thuringia.jpg" alt="Free State of Thuringia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Thuringia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1952 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Weimar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wilhelm Frick, Fritz Sauckel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Land Thüringen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=free-state-of-thuringia" aria-label="Free State of Thuringia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4930 409 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free State of Thuringia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5250 665L5257 666M5250 665L5283 666M5250 665L5238 678M5250 665L5300 622M5250 665L5300 622M5250 665L5258 673M5250 665L5246 655M5250 665L5275 669"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5257 666h0M5283 666h0M5238 678h0M5300 622h0M5300 622h0M5258 673h0M5246 655h0M5275 669h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5250 665h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5250 665h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot mark